 succeeded in 175ms:
   1: # SPEC: Phase 5 — Messaging Resilience
   2: 
   3: Goal: In the production messaging drawer, no message a user sends is lost or duplicated across network and 
Realtime outages, reloads, several tabs, account changes, and several devices; typing, unread/read state, and @mention 
notifications stay live and correct; and messaging health is observable through redacted logs — without regressing 
existing messaging.
   4: 
   5: Status: Final v4 — revised after adversarial reviews r2–r4 (`reviews/sol-review-spec-r2.md` … `r4.md`); r4 fixes 
applied without a fifth review by owner decision D7 (2026-10-06). Approved by the owner 2026-10-06 (native question 
UI: "Aprovada"). Interview rounds 1–2, confirmation, and owner decisions D1–D6 recorded in `state.md` (Verdict: Ready).
   6: 
   7: ## 1. Summary
   8: 
   9: The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, 
state in `MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency 
in `POST /api/messages/create`, stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and 
opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with 
server-fenced attempt generations and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; 
application-level recovery and health for every messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, 
members-only typing indicator; live cross-device unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including 
rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open 
conversation after reload.
  10: 
  11: ## 2. Problem statement
  12: 
  13: Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a send that fails 
must be retried by hand, a reload or closed tab discards it (the idempotency key lives only in component memory), and 
nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. Optimistic messages are matched to stored ones by guessing from 
content and time. Realtime recovery is incomplete: the SDK retries errored/timed-out channels and the 
conversation-list stream invalidates the list after its own rejoins, but the application exposes health only for the 
messages channel, does not recover terminal closures of the conversation-list channel, has no aggregate health, and 
has no polling fallback, so new messages and unread counts can silently stop updating. The typing indicator disappears 
after ~4 s of continuous typing and is broadcast on a public channel. Reading on one device leaves stale unread badges 
on the user's other devices. Nobody can mention someone, so room conversations (which never notify) cannot reach a 
specific person. Nobody can tell in production whether messaging is healthy.
  14: 
  15: ## 3. Goals and success metrics
  16: 
  17: ### Goals
  18: - G1: A text message the user sends is never silently lost: it is delivered, pending, or visibly failed with an 
action.
  19: - G2: No message is ever stored or shown twice because of automatic resends, several tabs, lost or late 
responses, or account changes; a message is never stored under a different sender.
  20: - G3: Messaging recovers from network and Realtime outages without user action and keeps updating through 
polling while Realtime is down.
  21: - G4: Typing indicators are reliable, named, and visible only to conversation members.
  22: - G5: Unread/read state and own sent messages stay in sync across the same user's tabs and devices (≤ 5 s while 
connectivity is healthy).
  23: - G6: A user can @mention conversation members, and the mentioned person is notified on the desktop, including 
in rooms.
  24: - G7: Messaging health (latency, delivery, connection) is observable in technical logs without exposing content.
  25: - G8: No existing messaging behavior regresses; every database change reaches the online target in a controlled, 
verified way at phase end.
  26: 
  27: ### Success metrics
  28: - With ≤ 10 pending messages and create quota available, all pending messages are delivered within 10 s after 
connectivity returns, without any click, in their original order, each exactly once; with more, delivery continues 
automatically within the server rate limit (D1).
  29: - With Realtime down and HTTP working, other members' new messages appear within 45 s of the outage start, and 
within 15 s (plus request time) of being sent once polling is active.
  30: - With healthy connectivity and Realtime, cross-device/tab sync of unread counts, read state, and own sent 
messages within 5 s (D4); during a Realtime outage the polling deadlines above apply.
  31: - Each flow is covered by a passing Playwright E2E test (two accounts, and two tabs / two devices where 
relevant) that passed 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge.
  32: - The existing messaging regression E2E suite passes after each slice.
  33: - Every migration of the phase is applied to the online target `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` at phase end with explicit 
owner authorization, catalog readback, and a runtime smoke check.
  34: - Owner final test (new app against the online database) accepts the phase.
  35: 
  36: ## 4. Users and stakeholders
  37: 
  38: ### Primary users
  39: - All workspace members who use the chat in direct, group, and room conversations (members of 
`conversation_members`), as sender, recipient, or mentioned user.
  40: 
  41: ### Secondary users
  42: - The same user on several tabs of one browser and on several devices/browsers at once; several users sharing 
one browser.
  43: - The owner/operator, who reads the messaging metric logs.
  44: 
  45: ### Stakeholders
  46: - Owner (Giuliano): approves the SPEC, performs the final test, authorizes the online migrations at phase end, 
and deploys the application.
  47: 
  48: ## 5. Scope
  49: 
  50: ### In scope
  51: - Durable outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies, written before 
dispatch; item states; cancel; per-conversation ordered automatic drain across tabs; claim leases and recovery; 
server-fenced attempts; identity fencing; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; session-end and sign-out 
rules; sending blocked with a clear message when durable storage is unavailable (D5).
  52: - Server support on create: sender fencing; attempt-generation fencing; key-based correlation (`clientMessageId` 
exposed to the sender in every message representation); insertion quota charged only for a winning insertion, plus an 
independent request budget for all create attempts and a separate budget for fence/lookup calls; mentions persisted 
atomically with the message and compared against the original composition on replay; a fence/lookup call by 
`clientMessageId` that never creates a message.
  53: - Connection banner in the drawer.
  54: - Application-level recovery, health, and catch-up for every messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open conversation).
  55: - Polling fallback after > 30 s of a required stream outage, while the tab is visible.
  56: - Typing indicator: continuous display, names, DM/group/room, private members-only channel, own sessions 
excluded, expiry.
  57: - Cross-device/tab sync of unread counters, read state, and own sent messages.
  58: - @mentions: autocomplete, highlighted mention, server-validated atomic mention records, desktop notification 
(DM, group, room, thread reply) authorized at emission time with browser-wide viewing suppression, feed highlight.
  59: - Messaging metrics as redacted structured server logs via an authenticated, rate-limited, size-bounded 
application route.
  60: - Phase 4 follow-ups: membership check inside `mark_conversation_read`; `get_unread_counts` not executable by 
`anon`; restore the open conversation after page reload.
  61: 
  62: ### Out of scope
  63: - Loading the application while offline (service worker / offline app shell) (D2).
  64: - Queuing attachments or voice notes (they keep the Phase 4 behavior).
  65: - Editing a pending message (cancel and rewrite instead).
  66: - Web Push / notifications with the browser or tab closed; cross-device notification suppression or dismissal.
  67: - `@todos` / `@here`; self-mentions; a separate mention-only notification preference; scrolling to the message 
on notification click.
  68: - Draft persistence; live sync of stars.
  69: - Usage/engagement metrics; a metrics UI or external analytics service.
  70: - Immediate revocation of typing signals for a tampered client of a removed member (accepted residual risk ≤ 
access-token lifetime, D3).
  71: - Safari and mobile browsers; drawer layout below ~400 px.
  72: - Other Phase 4 follow-ups: pending-upload cap, toaster covering the composer, pin no-op/English copy, orphan 
storage objects, "Favoritas" discoverability.
  73: - Deploying the application (owner action).
  74: 
  75: ## 6. User journeys
  76: 
  77: - J1 Offline send: A loses internet and keeps writing; each message appears as pending; the banner says "Sem 
conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar". Internet returns; with ≤ 10 pending and quota available, every 
pending message is delivered within 10 s in order and B sees each once; with more, delivery continues within the rate 
limit (D1).
  78: - J2 Reload/close: A has pending messages and reloads while offline (the browser shows its normal offline error) 
or closes the tab. When the page loads again with internet, the pending messages are there and are sent.
  79: - J3 Two tabs: A's pending message shows in both tabs as one item; only one tab sends a conversation's messages 
at a time; if that tab closes or hangs mid-send, the other finishes them once; cancelling a waiting message in either 
tab removes it from both.
  80: - J4 Rejection: A's pending message targets a room A has since lost access to; it becomes a visible failure with 
the text kept and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; later pending messages for that room wait; other conversations still 
deliver.
  81: - J5 Sign-out: A clicks Sair with 2 unsent messages; a dialog warns that leaving deletes them; Cancelar leaves A 
exactly where A was; Sair mesmo signs A out.
  82: - J6 Shared browser: A's session ends without Sair; C signs in on the same browser; C never sees A's messages 
and nothing is sent as C; when A signs in again, A's messages are sent as A.
  83: - J7 Realtime outage: after 5 s the banner shows "Reconectando…"; after 30 s the open conversation and the list 
update every 15 s; when Realtime returns, polling stops and the banner disappears.
  84: - J8 Typing: B types continuously in a group; A sees "Bruno está digitando…" the whole time; it disappears ≤ 5 s 
after B stops or leaves.
  85: - J9 Two devices: A reads part of conversation X on the laptop; within 5 s the desktop's badge and read state 
for X match.
  86: - J10 Mention: A types "@" in a room chat and picks Bruno; Bruno's tab is hidden; Bruno gets "Ana mencionou você 
em <sala>" with the full text; clicking opens the room; the message is highlighted for Bruno.
  87: - J11 Reload restore: A reloads with the drawer open on conversation X; the drawer reopens on X.
  88: 
  89: ## 7. Functional requirements
  90: 
  91: | ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance signal |
  92: |---|---|---|---|
  93: | FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written 
durably to the current user's outbox with its `clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and requested 
mention IDs, and only then dispatched and the composer cleared. If the durable write fails, nothing is dispatched 
(FR-033). The item shows in the feed as pending while not confirmed by the server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, AC-040 |
  94: | FR-002 | Pending items are sent automatically when connectivity returns (browser online, Realtime recovery, or 
periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, always with their own `clientMessageId`. With ≤ 10 pending and 
quota available, all are delivered within 10 s of connectivity returning; with more, delivery continues within the 
rate limit. A confirmed item becomes the normal sent message and leaves the outbox. | Must | AC-002, AC-009, AC-038 |
  95: | FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading while offline shows the browser's normal offline 
error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab 
of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
  96: | FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the 
oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). 
A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request 
timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt 
that started before or during the outage is superseded immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by 
whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the 
same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
  97: | FR-005 | Cancel: an item can be cancelled from any tab only if it is known not to be stored and no earlier 
attempt can still commit (never dispatched, last attempt definitively rejected, or fenced as not stored) and it is not 
claimed; the cancel is an atomic transition against claims — a cancelled item is never dispatched afterwards; a 
dispatched item shows "enviando" without cancel. | Must | AC-006 |
  98: | FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the item's `clientMessageId`. Every create and fence 
request carries the item's attempt generation (monotonic per key). The server accepts a create only if its generation 
is the current one for that (sender, conversation, key) at the moment of commit, and a fence with generation g 
atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as delivered) or makes every generation below g unable to 
commit — including requests still in the network that have not reached the server; a fence or create with a generation 
older than the server's current one is rejected. When an attempt ends without a usable response (network error, 
timeout, tab closed, lease expired, superseded, HTTP 5xx), the item is unknown; before any cancel, expiry, takeover, 
or new dispatch, the app fences it. The fence never creates a message. | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
  99: | FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its 
text and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the same conversation wait until it is resolved; other 
conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, "Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new 
`clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
 100: | FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays pending and is retried automatically, preserving 
order and without duplicates. The insertion quota is charged only for a winning insertion (including among concurrent 
requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored message do not consume it (D1). Independently, every 
create request (including replays, validation failures, and quota losers) consumes a per-user request budget, and 
fence/lookup calls consume their own per-user budget; exceeding any budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | AC-009 |
 101: | FR-009 | An item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send is never sent automatically: once fenced as not 
stored, it shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; an unknown item found stored resolves as 
delivered, never as expired. "Tentar de novo" on an expired item keeps the key and the original send time (for 
metrics) and starts a new 24 h delivery window, within which the normal transient/unknown rules apply. | Must | 
AC-010, AC-042 |
 102: | FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file 
error and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message whose uploads succeeded but whose create request 
fails keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must | AC-011 |
 103: | FR-011 | Identity fencing: each item belongs to an application user (`users.id`). Before every dispatch and 
fence call the app checks that the authenticated user equals the item's owner; every dispatch carries the expected 
sender, and the create and fence routes reject (without storing or fencing) a request whose authenticated user 
differs. When the authenticated user changes or signs out in any tab, all drains in all tabs stop; create, fence, and 
lookup responses that arrive afterwards are discarded: they never add content to another user's view, never resurrect 
deleted items, and never change a newer claim. Under the same identity, a response whose attempt generation is older 
than the item's current generation, or for an item already cancelled/deleted, is likewise ignored. | Must | AC-012, 
AC-044, AC-047 |
 104: | FR-012 | Session end without explicit sign-out: an item is held (not failed, not deleted) only when the 
client's auth state reports the session ended (signed out, or refresh rejected); a 401 while the client still holds a 
valid session is treated as transient. Held items drain after the same user signs in again (subject to FR-009); 
another user on the browser never sees, sends, or deletes them. | Must | AC-012 |
 105: | FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows 
"Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces 
no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and 
runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | 
Must | AC-013 |
 106: | FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports 
offline; "Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) 
has been unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must 
| AC-014 |
 107: | FR-015 | Every messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open 
conversation) has application-level health and recovery: after an error, timeout, or terminal closure it rejoins 
without user action using exponential backoff with a capped delay (in addition to the SDK's own retries); on recovery 
the messages and conversation-list streams trigger a catch-up refetch of the open feed and the list; the typing stream 
rejoins. | Must | AC-015 |
 108: | FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages, the receipt summaries of its loaded messages, any open 
reader-details list, and the conversation list every 15 s; on becoming visible while still unhealthy it fetches 
immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all streams are healthy no new polling request starts. 
Merging polling and Realtime results never duplicates a message and never replaces newer or enriched state 
(attachments, mentions, reactions, receipts, loaded history pages) with older data. | Must | AC-016, AC-048 |
 109: | FR-017 | The typing indicator stays visible while the other person types continuously and disappears at most 5 
s after they stop, blur the composer, send, leave the conversation, or close the tab. | Must | AC-017 |
 110: | FR-018 | The indicator names typists: 1 → "Ana está digitando…", 2 → "Ana e Bruno estão digitando…", 3+ → "N 
pessoas estão digitando…", in direct, group, and room conversations. | Must | AC-018 |
 111: | FR-019 | Typing signals travel only on a private Realtime topic whose join and send are authorized for current 
conversation members; anon clients, authenticated non-members, and public-topic attempts never receive them; the app 
detects the user's own removal from a conversation through a signal that does not depend on still having access to 
that conversation and leaves the topic within 5 s. Payload: user identity and typing flag only. Residual risk accepted 
(D3, D6): a tampered client of a removed member may receive and send typing signals until its access token expires. | 
Must | AC-019 |
 112: | FR-020 | A user never sees their own typing indicator, including from their other tabs or devices. | Must | 
AC-020 |
 113: | FR-021 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, within 5 s and without reload, focus change, or 
reconnect: when the user reads messages (fully or partly) on one tab/device, their other tabs/devices show the same 
