 169: 
 170: ### Constraints

exec
"C:\\WINDOWS\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command '$reviewLines = Get-Content -Encoding utf8 
-LiteralPath '"'spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md'; for ("'$reviewIdx = 170; $reviewIdx -lt 
$reviewLines.Count; $reviewIdx++) { '"'{0,4}: {1}' -f ("'$reviewIdx + 1), $reviewLines[$reviewIdx] }' in 
E:\projects\virtual-office
 succeeded in 114ms:
 171: - Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase, TanStack Query; reuse existing hooks, repositories, routes, 
and query keys rather than parallel implementations.
 172: - Identity: RLS compares `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text`; foreign keys use `users.id`; messages 
link to rooms through `conversations.room_id`.
 173: - Browsers: Chrome and Edge desktop (automated E2E); Firefox desktop (verified). Safari and mobile not required.
 174: - No service worker; background notifications are page-level only.
 175: - No message text, names, or identifiers in metrics (BR-013); no external analytics service.
 176: - The sign-out confirmation must not change the Presence logout sequence (presence-safety rules apply to that 
touch point).
 177: - Online database changes only at phase end with explicit authorization (BR-014); the hosted app currently runs 
the pre-Phase-4 build until the owner deploys, so new server contracts must stay compatible with that build and with 
the Phase 4 build until deployment: unkeyed legacy message writes and existing RPC/route signatures keep working 
against the migrated database.
 178: - Repository test policy (BR-015); type-check, lint, and build must pass.
 179: - Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) and adjustable in planning only with owner 
approval: claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch ≤ 50 events / 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 
s metrics abort.
 180: 
 181: ### Assumptions
 182: - None beyond the user-confirmed answers and owner decisions recorded in `state.md`.
 183: 
 184: ## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
 185: 
 186: | Case | Expected behavior |
 187: |---|---|
 188: | Tab closed right after pressing send, before server confirmation | Item is already in the outbox; on next load 
it is fenced and delivered once (AC-040). |
 189: | Request stored but response lost | Item becomes unknown; fence finds it stored; resolves as delivered; never 
offered for cancel or expired (AC-038, AC-042). |
 190: | Original create blocked before its INSERT, or still in the network, while another tab fences | The fence wins; 
the older generation can no longer commit; the item is resent once or cancelled/expired without a late duplicate 
(AC-045). |
 191: | Stale fence arrives after a takeover | Rejected as an older generation; the newer attempt is unaffected 
(AC-045). |
 192: | Hung holder when connectivity returns | Its attempt is superseded at once; D1 deadline holds (AC-041). |
 193: | Two tabs come online together | One drain claim per conversation; only the oldest unresolved item is eligible; 
no overlapping create requests for that conversation; each stored and shown once, in order (AC-005). |
 194: | Owning tab closes or hangs mid-request | Closed or lease expired: another tab fences and finishes once; while 
the lease is live no other tab dispatches (AC-041). |
 195: | Cancel races a claim | Exactly one wins; a cancelled item is never dispatched; a dispatched item cannot be 
cancelled (AC-006). |
 196: | Lost access before reconnect | First item fails definitively with text; later items of that conversation wait; 
other conversations deliver (AC-007). |
 197: | Access regained after failure | "Tentar de novo" delivers it, then the waiting items in order (AC-008). |
 198: | Other definitive outcomes (404, 400, 413, 422) and 5xx | Definitive → failed with text; 5xx → unknown → fence 
(AC-046). |
 199: | `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` | Failed; "Tentar de novo" resends the content under a new key, once (AC-043). |
 200: | Server rate limit during drain; concurrent same-key requests | Items stay pending, retried automatically; 
quota charged once per stored message; replays/fences use a separate budget (AC-009). |
 201: | Laptop asleep > 24 h | Not-stored items become "expirada"; stored ones resolve as delivered (AC-010, AC-042). |
 202: | Offline attachment / voice | Upload failure: per-file error + retry; create failure after upload: Phase 4 
composer failure; nothing in the outbox (AC-011). |
 203: | IndexedDB unavailable or write fails | Nothing dispatched; text stays in the composer with the storage message 
(D5) (AC-039). |
 204: | Identical texts sent twice; Realtime arrives before the HTTP response | One bubble per message, matched by key 
(AC-050). |
 205: | Account switched while a drain is paused or a response is in flight | Drain stops; no dispatch as the new 
user; late responses discarded; server rejects any request whose sender differs (AC-044, AC-047). |
 206: | Session ends without Sair; other user signs in | Other user never sees/sends A's items; A's items drain after 
A signs in (AC-012). |
 207: | Temporary Auth failure (401 with valid client session) | Treated as transient; retried; not held or deleted 
(AC-012). |
 208: | Sair with unsent items | Dialog; Cancelar has no side effects at any entry point; Sair mesmo stops drains 
everywhere and deletes the outbox; late responses do not resurrect items (AC-013, AC-047). |
 209: | Realtime drop < 5 s | No "Reconectando…" banner (AC-014). |
 210: | Only the conversation-list stream fails | Banner after 5 s and polling after 30 s follow its health; recovered 
independently (AC-015, AC-016). |
 211: | Stale poll returns after a newer Realtime update | Newer/enriched state kept; no duplicates (AC-048). |
 212: | Realtime down > 30 s, tab hidden | No polling; on visible, fetch immediately then every 15 s (AC-016). |
 213: | Typist closes the tab or leaves the conversation mid-typing | Indicator disappears ≤ 5 s (AC-017). |
 214: | Anon, outsider, public-topic, or removed-member typing access | Refused / no events; removed member's app 
leaves at once; tampered client bounded by D3 (AC-019). |
 215: | Forged mention IDs, plain "@name", changed mentions on replay, recipient removed before replay | No mention 
for non-members/self/plain text; changed composition → 409; unchanged replay returns the stored message (AC-024, 
AC-046). |
 216: | Recipient removed or account switched after commit but before the notification is shown | No notification once 
the app has the removal/switch signal; a removal not yet received at the final check is the accepted D6 race (AC-049). 
|
 217: | Manual retry of an expired item hits 429 or an unknown outcome | New 24 h window: retried/fenced automatically 
(AC-010). |
 218: | Partial reads by others during a Realtime-only outage | Receipt summaries and open reader details update via 
polling (AC-016). |
 219: | Hidden tab receives a mention event first while another tab views that conversation | No notification 
(AC-026). |
 220: | Malicious, oversized, hanging, or sustained metrics traffic | Rejected or dropped; route rate-limited; 
messaging unaffected (AC-028, AC-029). |
 221: | Restored conversation no longer accessible | Conversation list opens without error (AC-032). |
 222: 
 223: ## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
 224: - Outbox content lives only in the user's browser, scoped to the application user ID, fenced against account 
switches on the client and on the server (expected-sender check on create and fence), and deleted on confirmed 
explicit sign-out.
 225: - The fence/lookup call acts only on the caller's own message for that key, never creates a message, and is 
rejected for anon users and for other users' keys.
 226: - Every create request consumes a per-user request budget and fence/lookup calls their own, so the 
insertion-quota exemption for replays (D1) cannot be used for unlimited traffic.
 227: - Typing topics are private and members-only for join and send (Realtime RLS with `users.supabase_uid = 
auth.uid()::text` → `users.id`); the app leaves within 5 s of a removal; residual receive/send exposure of tampered 
removed-member clients accepted (D3, D6).
 228: - Mentions are validated and stored server-side in the message transaction; clients cannot write them directly; 
only members can read them; notifications are re-authorized right before emission (residual race accepted, D6).
 229: - Notifications never leak content of conversations the user is not (or no longer) a member of.
 230: - Metrics: closed enums, bounded numbers and sizes, server-derived version, per-user rate limit, no free text 
(BR-013); unauthenticated requests are rejected and produce no metric log line.
 231: - `mark_conversation_read` membership check and `anon` revoke on `get_unread_counts` close two Phase 4 findings.
 232: - No service-role key in client code; service-role use never replaces application authorization checks.
 233: - Every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API route change passes the Supabase/RLS review gate.
 234: 
 235: ## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
 236: - UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out dialog text, typing phrases, and mention 
notification title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", "enviando", "expirada"), actions 
("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar"), are proposed (the storage message in FR-033 is the owner's D5 wording), and 
equivalent Portuguese wording with the same meaning is accepted (D4).
 237: - Connection banner uses a polite live region; item states have text labels, not only icons.
 238: - Item actions and the sign-out dialog are keyboard-operable with visible focus; the dialog traps focus and Esc 
means Cancelar.
 239: - The mention list follows the combobox/listbox pattern and is announced to screen readers.
 240: - The mention highlight does not rely on color alone.
 241: 
 242: ## 14. Acceptance criteria
 243: 
 244: - AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed 
shows it as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
 245: - AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages offline in conversation X with quota available, 
when connectivity returns, then within 10 s and without any click B sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the 
original order, and A's items become normal sent messages.
 246: - AC-003 (FR-001, FR-006, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network 
error after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is fenced, and is delivered exactly once without user action once 
requests succeed.
 247: - AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads while offline, then the browser's offline error is 
shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. 
Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app later delivers it once.
 248: - AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in 
conversation X in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously and m1's real 
create request is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until m1 is resolved; the network logs of both 
tabs show no overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, exactly once each; the database holds one row 
per key.
 249: - AC-006 (FR-005, BR-007): Given a waiting item never dispatched, when A cancels it in tab 2 at the moment tab 1 
tries to claim it, then either the cancel wins (the item disappears from both tabs and no create request for it is 
ever sent) or the claim wins (no cancel action is offered and the item is delivered once); given a dispatched item, it 
shows "enviando" and offers no cancel.
 250: - AC-007 (FR-007, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost 
access to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 shows failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" / 
"Apagar", m2 stays waiting with no create request sent, and m3 is delivered.
 251: - AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, 
then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 
is attempted next.
 252: - AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, 
then rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B finally sees all 12 in order, once each. On the real 
route: a replay of an already-stored message does not reduce the insertion quota; replays, validation failures, and 
quota losers all consume the request budget and are rejected (429) once it is exhausted; fence/lookup calls are 
limited by their own budget; concurrent requests for one new key charge the insertion quota once; concurrent distinct 
keys at the quota boundary store no more messages than the quota allows.
 253: - AC-010 (FR-009, BR-006): Given a never-dispatched item whose send time is more than 24 h ago, when 
connectivity returns, then no create request is sent for it and it shows "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; 
"Tentar de novo" delivers it once; given a manual retry of an expired item that gets 429 and then an unknown outcome, 
it is retried/fenced automatically and stored once.
 254: - AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a file, then its upload shows the existing per-file 
error and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; given A's uploads succeeded and then the network 
drops, when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing 
enters the outbox.
 255: - AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): Given A has unsent items and A's browser session 
ends without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token revoked so the client reports signed out), when C 
signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no create or fence request is sent while C is 
authenticated; when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as A. Given a 401 while A's client session is 
valid, then the item stays pending and is retried.
 256: - AC-013 (FR-013, BR-008): Given A has 2 unsent items, when A clicks Sair from each in-app entry point, then the 
dialog shows "Você tem 2 mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens."; Cancelar sends no logout request, shows 
no notice, and does not navigate; Sair mesmo with a drain in flight in another tab stops all drains, deletes the 
outbox, and signs out with the Presence logout sequence as before; after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 
unsent items, Sair signs out without a dialog.
 257: - AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão 
enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when the messages 
stream alone, and separately the conversation-list stream alone, stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" 
appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without user action.
 258: - AC-015 (FR-015): Given each required stream is dropped independently (messages stream only; conversation-list 
stream only, including a terminal closure; typing stream only), when Realtime becomes available again, then that 
stream recovers without user action; B's messages sent during the outage appear once; unread counts are correct; 
typing works again; recorded recovery events show increasing, capped retry delays.
 259: - AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, 
when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 45 s; a message B sends after polling started 
appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update; when C reads part of A's earlier messages 
during the outage (no new message sent), A's receipt summaries and open reader-details list update within 15 s plus 
request time once polling is active. Given only the conversation-list stream is unhealthy for more than 30 s, polling 
starts too. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests are made; when it becomes visible, a fetch happens 
immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the message delivered by both appears once, and no new 
polling request starts during the next 60 s.
 260: - AC-017 (FR-017; must-not R1-q16 E): Given B types continuously for 15 s, then A sees the typing indicator at 
every 1 s sample; when B stops, it disappears within 5 s; when B closes the tab mid-typing, within 5 s; when B 
switches to another conversation mid-typing, within 5 s.
 261: - AC-018 (FR-018): Given a group or room with A, B, C, D, when B types, A sees "<B> está digitando…"; B and C → 
"<B> e <C> estão digitando…"; B, C, D → "3 pessoas estão digitando…"; a direct conversation shows the single-typist 
phrase.
 262: - AC-019 (FR-019, BR-009): With real local Realtime: an anon client, an authenticated non-member, and a client 
on the public topic name receive no typing events of conversation X and cannot send typing events members receive; 
member A receives B's typing; when member E is removed from X while subscribed (and while X is not open in E's 
drawer), E's app leaves the topic within 5 s and shows no further typing for X (integration + E2E).
 263: - AC-020 (FR-020, BR-009): Given A has two tabs and a second device on X, when A types in tab 1, then neither 
tab 2 nor the second device shows A as typing.
 264: - AC-021 (FR-021): Given healthy connectivity and A on two devices with 6 unread in X, when A reads only the 
first 3 on device 1, then within 5 s device 2 shows 3 unread for X; after reading the rest, 0. Given A is the sender 
of messages read by B, then A's receipt summary/details on both of A's sessions match the stored receipts within 5 s.
 265: - AC-022 (FR-022): Given healthy connectivity and A on two devices, when A sends on device 1, then it appears 
once on device 2 within 5 s without reload.
 266: - AC-023 (FR-023, BR-010): Given A types "@" in a room conversation, then the list shows only room-conversation 
members other than A, filters as A types, and supports arrows/Enter/Tab/Esc and mouse; choosing "Bruno" inserts a 
highlighted mention; a workspace user who never joined the room chat is not listed.
 267: - AC-024 (FR-023, FR-024, BR-010; must-not R1-q16 F): On the real route and local DB: a message mentioning a 
non-member ID and the sender's own ID stores no mention for either and notifies neither; a replay that changes only 
the requested mention IDs returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` and changes nothing; a message whose text contains 
"@Bruno" typed without selection stores no mention; a forced failure of mention storage stores neither the message nor 
any mention; anon and non-members cannot read mention records; no client can insert/update/delete them directly.
 268: - AC-025 (FR-025, BR-011): Given B has notifications enabled and permission granted, B's tab hidden or viewing 
another conversation, when A mentions B in a room, then B receives exactly one notification "<A> mencionou você em 
<sala>" with the full text; clicking opens that room in the drawer; a room message without a mention of B produces 
none.
 269: - AC-026 (FR-025, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 C): Given B has notifications enabled, then a DM mention yields 
exactly one notification; a thread-reply mention yields one; with B's two tabs where the hidden tab receives the event 
first and the visible tab is viewing that conversation, none; A's own message yields none for A; with the toggle off, 
none; a mention across B's two hidden tabs yields exactly one.
 270: - AC-027 (FR-026): Given A's message mentions B, then all members see the mention styled distinctly, and only B 
sees the whole message highlighted with a non-color cue.
 271: - AC-028 (FR-027, BR-013): Given a send, a stream recovery, and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route 
writes structured lines with enum event type/reason, durations, and server-derived version; a scan finds no message 
text, names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; a payload with unknown fields, free-text strings, out-of-range numbers, 
or a forged version produces no such values in the logs; a request over 50 events or 16 KB is rejected; requests 
beyond the per-user rate limit are rejected; an unauthenticated request is rejected and produces no metric log line.
 272: - AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and separately hangs, and separately is flooded with 
sustained traffic, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold, at most 
one metrics request is in flight from A's tab, a hanging metrics request is aborted after 5 s, the client buffer never 
exceeds its bound (oldest events dropped), and no error is shown.
 273: - AC-030 (FR-029): Given user E is not a member of X, when `mark_conversation_read` is invoked for E and X 
through every role that can execute it, then the call is refused and E's read state for X is unchanged (integration, 
local DB).
 274: - AC-031 (FR-030): Given the `anon` role, when it calls `get_unread_counts`, then permission is denied; an 
authenticated member still gets correct counts (integration, local DB).
 275: - AC-032 (FR-031, BR-016): Given the drawer open on X, when A reloads, then it reopens on X; given A lost access 
to X, then the list opens with no error; given C signs in on that browser, then C does not get A's conversation; given 
the drawer was closed, it stays closed.
 276: - AC-033 (FR-032): The existing Phase 4 messaging E2E regression suite and DB integration suite pass after each 
slice; `npm run type-check`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run build` pass.
 277: - AC-034 (BR-014, §10): Every migration of the phase is applied locally with recorded rollback SQL; before any 
online application, the currently hosted build (git ref confirmed by the owner) and the Phase 4 build each pass a 
send/read/unread smoke against the migrated local database (legacy unkeyed writes still work); at phase end, after 
explicit owner authorization, it is applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`, the catalog is read back from that target, and 
a runtime smoke check passes; the hand-off states the app is ready to deploy after the migrations.
 278: - AC-035 (§12): The Supabase/RLS review gate passes for every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and 
API route change.
 279: - AC-036 (BR-015): Each new E2E spec passed 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and in Edge; the key flows (outbox, 
banner, typing, mentions) were verified in Firefox and recorded; no unit test was added; every touched unit test was 
replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
 280: - AC-037 (FR-014, §13): The banner is announced via a polite live region, item states have text labels, and the 
sign-out dialog and mention list are fully keyboard-operable.
 281: - AC-038 (FR-002, FR-006, BR-004): Given A's create request is stored but its response is dropped, then the item 
becomes unknown, the fence resolves it as delivered without a second create, and B sees it once.
 282: - AC-039 (FR-033, R1-q2 A, D5): Given IndexedDB is unavailable (and separately a failing outbox write), when A 
sends a text message, then zero create requests are sent, the text and reply target stay in the composer, and "Este 
navegador não permite guardar mensagens; libere o armazenamento do site" is shown.
 283: - AC-040 (FR-001): Given A is online and the create request is held, when A closes the tab before the server 
confirms, then on reopening the app the item is fenced and B sees the message exactly once.
 284: - AC-041 (FR-004): Given tab 1 holds the claim and its create request is held, then tab 2 sends no create 
request for that conversation while tab 1's lease is live; when tab 1 is closed (and separately when its lease expires 
while it hangs), tab 2 fences and the message is stored once. Given tab 1's attempt hangs across an outage, when 
connectivity returns with that single item pending and quota available, then it is delivered within 10 s (D1) and 
stored once.
 285: - AC-042 (FR-006, FR-009): Given an unknown item whose request was actually stored, when A tries to cancel it or 
its 24 h passes, then no cancel is offered before fencing, the fence resolves it as delivered, and it never shows 
"expirada".
 286: - AC-043 (FR-007): Given an item rejected with `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, when A clicks "Tentar de novo", then 
its content is stored once under a new key and the conflicting stored message is unchanged.
 287: - AC-044 (FR-011, BR-001, §12): Given A's drain is paused before dispatch and the browser's session switches to 
C (a member of the same conversation), when the drain would resume, then no create or fence request is sent as C; a 
crafted create or fence request carrying A as expected sender with C's session is rejected and stores or fences 
nothing; an anon fence/lookup is rejected; C fencing or looking up a key that belongs to A's message (with C as 
sender) neither returns A's message nor affects A's attempts.
 288: - AC-045 (FR-006, BR-004): With real local Postgres and the real routes: (a) attempt 1's INSERT is blocked (row 
lock held by the test) while a fence for that key reports not stored; on release attempt 1 does not commit; (b) 
attempt 1's request is held before reaching the server while a fence runs; when released it is rejected as stale; (c) 
a stale fence (older generation) arriving after a newer attempt was dispatched is rejected and the newer attempt 
commits; in every case at most one message is stored (integration + E2E).
 289: - AC-046 (FR-007, FR-024, BR-005): On the real route: 403 (missing conversation or not a participant), 400 
(invalid reply target), and 413 (content too large) are classified definitive in the drawer (failed with text); a 5xx 
returned after the request reached the server is classified unknown and fenced; an unchanged replay after a mentioned 
recipient left the conversation returns the stored message (not 409) and notifies no removed recipient.
 290: - AC-047 (FR-011, FR-013): Given a real create response and a real fence response held while the browser 
switches from A to C, and separately while A confirms Sair mesmo, and separately (same identity) while the item is 
cancelled or superseded by a newer generation, when the responses are released, then no A content appears in C's view, 
no deleted or cancelled item reappears, and no newer claim or attempt is changed.
 291: - AC-048 (FR-016): Given a poll response is held while Realtime delivers a newer state (a reaction, a receipt, 
an attachment message, a mention) and older history pages are loaded, when the stale poll is released, then the feed 
keeps the newer state, the loaded pages, and one bubble per message.
 292: - AC-049 (FR-025, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 F): Given B is mentioned and B's authoritative check has already 
returned "member" but display is held, when B's app receives B's removal signal (and separately when the browser 
switches to another account) before display, then no notification is shown; given the check is held before it runs and 
B is removed, then no notification is shown.
 293: - AC-050 (FR-034): Given A sends two messages with identical text (one plain, one replying to a message) and 
Realtime delivers each before its HTTP response, then each shows as exactly one bubble in both of A's tabs throughout, 
matched by `clientMessageId`.
 294: 
 295: ## 15. Test strategy
 296: 
 297: - Test levels:
 298:   - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API routes): sender and attempt-generation fencing 
incl. stale requests and stale fences (AC-044, AC-045), insertion quota, request budget, and fence budget (AC-009), 
fence/lookup authorization incl. anon and other users' keys (AC-038, AC-042, AC-044), outcome classes (AC-046), 
mention atomicity/validation/replay/RLS (AC-024, AC-046), private typing-topic authorization incl. 
anon/outsider/public topic (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), `get_unread_counts` anon revoke 
(AC-031), metrics route auth, enums, sizes, rate limit, redaction (AC-028).
 299:   - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two tabs of one account; a second device as a separate 
browser context): every other AC. Network shaping only — the app, routes, and Supabase stay real: browser-context 
offline mode; blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a single stream; holding a request or response by delayed 
pass-through to the real server; dropping a response or replacing it with a gateway 5xx after pass-through; disabling 
IndexedDB or failing its writes in the page (AC-039). Old-build compatibility: the hosted and Phase 4 builds are run 
locally against the migrated database (AC-034). Expiry may use a test-controlled clock without changing the product 
rule.
 300:   - Unit: none (BR-015).
 301: - Tests trace to acceptance criteria by AC ID; written from the ACs before or with the implementation.
 302: - Test data: accounts A, B, C, D, E in one company; a DM, a group, and a room conversation; a room the test can 
remove A/E from and re-add; a way to exhaust A's create quota locally.
 303: - Environments: local Supabase for integration/E2E; Chrome and Edge for automated E2E; Firefox verification of 
key flows recorded; online target only for phase-end readback and smoke (BR-014).
 304: - Reliability: each new E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing runs (BR-015).
 305: - Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream 
Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and stale polls, typing tab close and conversation 
switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification 
race, delayed notification authorization, malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost 
access.
 306: 
 307: ## 16. Validation and launch checklist
 308: 
 309: - All Must FRs and AC-001..AC-050 have evidence in TRACK.
 310: - Supabase/RLS review gate passed (AC-035).
 311: - Type-check, lint, build, integration, E2E, and the regression suite pass; new E2E specs passed 3 consecutive 
runs in Chrome and Edge; Firefox verification recorded.
 312: - Phase-end migrations applied online with authorization, readback, and smoke recorded (AC-034).
 313: - Owner final test of the new app against the online database with two accounts.
 314: - Hand-off states the application is ready to deploy after the migrations; deployment is performed by the owner.
